01-d09f-5472-8fc1-6a897d208963"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-765bd662-67d2-591a-8640-11d80d7e703d" :answer/id #uuid "765bd662-67d2-591a-8640-11d80d7e703d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[0]_{11}\\cup [1]_{11}\\cup ⋯\\cup [10]_{11}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90b93201-d09f-5472-8fc1-6a897d208963" :answer/id #uuid "90b93201-d09f-5472-8fc1-6a897d208963" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{[0]_{11},[1]_{11},…,[10]_{11}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153456 :tutorial/content "Given an integer $a,$ the **residue class** (or **congruence class**) of $a$ modulo $n$ is the set containing all integers congruent to $a$ (modulo $n$). \n\nThe residue class of $a$ modulo $n,$ denoted $[a]_n,$ can be expressed using a conditional definition as follows:\n\n$$\n[a]_n = \\big\\{ x \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: | \\: x \\equiv a \\,  \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nPut another way, the residue class $[a]_n$ contains all integers that have the same remainder as $a$ when divided by $n.$\n\nFor example, the residue class of $0$ modulo $2$ contains all the even integers because they all have a remainder of zero when divided by $2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0]_{2} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)} \\\\  & ={2k\\,|\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,-4,-2,0,2,4…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe residue class of $1$ modulo $2$ contains all the odd integers because they all have a remainder of one when divided by $2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[1]_{2} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)} \\\\  & ={2k+1\\,|\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,-3,-1,1,3,5…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that each element of $\\mathbb Z$ lies either in $[0]_2$ or in $[1]_2$ (but never both). Thus, these residue classes form a *partition* of the integers. We'll call this partition $\\mathbb Z_2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Z_2 = \\big\\{[0]_2, [1]_2\\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe can visualize this partition using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f84b296319e078df1827fe29dbd15fb61ce0d10cac69660f11d6a96d5c44478a.png)\n\nSimilarly, the integers can also be partitioned into five residue classes modulo $5.$ \n\n$$\n\\mathbb Z_5 = \\big\\{[0]_5, [1]_5, [2]_5,[3]_5, [4]_5 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, each residue class contains all numbers that are congruent modulo $5.$ We can visualize this partition of $\\mathbb Z$ using the diagram below.\n\n![](images/27/270f63afd4a0022e9f31a48e5ea5f4e799822e8c4eb5813e6c5e616e316c02e1.png)\n\nA residue class is a specific type of a more general object called an **equivalence class.** We'll learn more about different types of equivalence classes soon." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153479 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to our diagram for visualizing the residue classes modulo ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg)\n\n![](images/f8/f84b296319e078df1827fe29dbd15fb61ce0d10cac69660f11d6a96d5c44478a.png)\n\nAny given representation of a residue class is not unique.\n\nFor example, the integers congruent to zero modulo ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) are usually denoted ![](images/7e/7e03abb8339cac8bc90200b99c4de9c414e179036e7b7defca80f7388c9fdb7c.svg) In this case, ![](images/15/156b31ede73de5301befe67f02745a0202f1806a62f9fdecc6fc9ee3f21117ba.svg) is the **representative** of the class:\n![](images/5f/5f298c564c0538edb6fc85abb585f5fef0b0bcff7fa704860794bb5681fb036d.svg)\n\nHowever, we can use *any* element of the class to represent the class. Here are some examples:\n\n![](images/38/383a513e20a70007e3d102dd4c8e9898550ac30514a3352842e1b9be8ad7601e.svg)\n\nWe can also use negative numbers as representatives of the class:\n\n![](images/01/014ec5b5f21d3b216343811bddb6d84c3a66c7b29baa09504d67cab71b83aea6.svg)\n\nWhat's important to understand is that *all of these sets are equal*:\n\n![](images/31/3155c29054f13a2b96de8be909ebd1ac6c98d50f64ea5d7a0acef3f1dcf48ac1.svg)\n\nThe same idea is true for ![](images/7a/7ac9fdeb690307bfa97c781ae9ace4d91bbf6f3b15cfe6dd7139225bdca44fc5.svg)\n\n![](images/cd/cd1101d39e228d23c551fcaa8ba377cd819661aaec6121706eab184658d2a854.svg)\n\nThe same idea applies to any residue class modulo ![](images/c2/c2475d72e6ec36042292de6c0160930174172b2386148d823a70e59fddfb93c2.svg) For example:\n\n![](images/0c/0c4d211a3b3f6238f9e647585d62904af4fd263fb79eb94e45e9b51d8b62abde.svg)\n\nDespite this non-uniqueness, we usually choose the smallest nonnegative element of a residue class to represent the class." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153459 :tutorial/content "Let's list some important properties of residue classes:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ *distinct* residue classes modulo $n.$\n\n- If $a \\equiv b \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n)$ then $[a]_n=[b]_n.$ In particular, this allows us to pick any element of a class as its representative.\n\n- On the other hand, two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: \n\n$$\na \\not\\equiv b \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:n)\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n[a]_n \\cap [b]_n = \\emptyset\n$$\n\n- The residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers. This means the integers can be constructed from the (disjoint) union of all distinct residue classes. \n\n$$\n\\mathbb{Z} = [0]_n \\cup [1]_n \\cup \\cdots \\cup [n-1]_n\n$$\n\nThe **integers modulo** $\\boldsymbol n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n\\mathbb{Z}_n = \\big\\{[0]_n,\\, [1]_n,\\, \\ldots,\\,  [n-1]_n\\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe set $\\mathbb Z_n$ has some interesting properties. We'll learn more about those in future lessons." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152339 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
